name: redirection
boss: DevNull
flavor: Your stderr falls into the void.
syntax: 2>&1, >&2, 2>, >>, >, <, |, '...'
brief: A command has three channels: stdin (0) it reads, stdout (1) it prints,
brief: and stderr (2) it complains on. Redirection re-points them at files.
brief:
brief: `>` truncates the target first. `>>` appends. That single character is the
brief: difference between adding today's log and destroying yesterday's, and
brief: no confirmation stands between you and it.
brief:
brief: `2>` catches errors only. `> file 2>&1` catches both, in that order --
brief: `2>&1 > file` does not, because the duplication happens before the move.
brief:
brief: `|` wires one command's stdout into the next's stdin -- `tee`, later in
brief: this level, needs it.
brief:
brief: The commands either side are ones you have: `ls`, `grep`, `sort`, `wc`, `echo`.
brief: `printf` is the new one, and the better one -- it prints exactly the
brief: format you hand it, no newline unless you write `\n` yourself.
---
q: {write @word@ into @word@.txt with `echo`, replacing what's there|write @word@ into @word@.txt with `echo`, overwriting what was before|save @word@ to @word@.txt with `echo`, wiping out old contents}
a: echo @word@ > @word@.txt
state: match:@word@.txt:@word@
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: > creates or truncates the file, then writes
---
q: {append the word @word@ to @txt@ with `echo`, keeping the rest|add the word @word@ to the end of @txt@ with `echo`, intact|tack the word @word@ onto @txt@ with `echo`, wiping nothing}
a: echo @word@ >> @txt@
state: match:@txt@:rotate
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: two angle brackets, not one
---
q: {feed @txt@ into `sort`'s stdin with a redirect, not a filename|connect @txt@ to `sort`'s stdin with a redirect, not an argument|feed @txt@ into `sort` via <, not as a filename argument}
a: sort < @txt@
out: ask why the fan
ref: sort
teach: sort
hint: < connects the file to stdin -- sort never sees a filename argument
---
q: {run "`ls` @word@-missing" and send only its error message to `errors.txt`|run "`ls` @word@-missing", capturing just the error message in `errors.txt`|run "`ls` @word@-missing" so only the error text lands in `errors.txt`}
a: ls @word@-missing 2> errors.txt
state: match:errors.txt:No such file
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: the error channel is number 2
---
q: {run "`ls` @word@-missing" and throw its error message away entirely|run "`ls` @word@-missing", discarding its error message completely|run "`ls` @word@-missing" so its error message vanishes entirely}
a: ls @word@-missing 2> /dev/null
status: 1
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: /dev/null is the bit bucket -- everything written to it is discarded
---
q: {run "`ls` @dir@ @word@-missing", output and errors both into `all.txt`|run "`ls` @dir@ @word@-missing", capturing output and errors in `all.txt`|run "`ls` @dir@ @word@-missing" so everything lands in `all.txt`}
a: ls @dir@ @word@-missing > all.txt 2>&1
state: match:all.txt:No such file
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: redirect stdout first, then point stderr at wherever stdout now goes
---
q: %show% @log@ on screen while saving a copy to `copy.txt`, with `tee`
a: cat @log@ | tee copy.txt
out: service started
ref: tee
teach: tee
hint: tee writes to the file and passes the same data through to stdout
---
q: %show% @log@ on screen and APPEND a copy to `copy.txt`, with `tee`
a: cat @log@ | tee -a copy.txt
out: service started
ref: tee
teach: tee -a
hint: without -a, tee truncates the file the way > does
---
q: %count% @txt@'s lines and write the number into @cvar@.txt, with `wc`
a: wc -l < @txt@ > @cvar@.txt
a: wc -l @txt@ > @cvar@.txt
state: match:@cvar@.txt:^[0-9]
ref: wc
teach: wc -l
hint: redirecting in with < keeps the filename out of wc's output
---
q: %show% the word @word@ with `printf` -- no surprises like echo has
a: printf '%s\n' @word@
out: ^@word@$
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: printf takes a format first, then the values to fill it with
---
q: %show% the word @word@ with `printf` and no trailing newline
a: printf '%s' @word@
out: @word@
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: printf only outputs what's in the format string -- no automatic newline
---
q: %show% @word@ and @term@ on one tab-separated line, with `printf`
a: printf '%s\t%s\n' @word@ @term@
out: @word@
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: \t in the format string is a literal tab
---
q: %show% @n@ as a zero-padded, five-digit id, with `printf`
a: printf '%05d\n' @n@
out: ^0*[0-9]+$
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: the 0 before the 5 means zero-pad instead of space-pad
---
q: {%show% the word @word@ on stderr, not stdout, with `echo`|write the word @word@ to stderr, not stdout, with `echo`|`echo` @word@ so it lands on the error stream, not the output one}
a: echo @word@ >&2
out: @word@
ref: echo
teach: echo
hint: >&2 points stdout at wherever stderr goes -- short for 1>&2, since 1 is the default
---
setup: "ls @dir@ @word@-missing" -- one real hit, one error
q: {run it with stderr merged in, then `grep` for "`such`"|run it, merging stderr with `2>&1`, then `grep` "`such`"|run it with output and errors merged, then `grep` "`such`"}
a: ls @dir@ @word@-missing 2>&1 | grep such
out: such
ref: grep
teach: grep
hint: 2>&1 before the pipe merges stderr into the same stream grep reads -- 2>&1 after the pipe would be too late
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: echo one > f; echo two > f; cat f
a: two
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: > truncates the file each time it runs, so only the last write survives
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: echo one > f; echo two >> f; wc -l < f
a: 2
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: >> adds onto what's there instead of truncating it like > does
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: echo hi 2>/dev/null
a: hi
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: 2>/dev/null only redirects the error channel -- stdout still comes through
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: : > f; ls f nope > out.txt 2>&1 | wc -l
a: 0
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: > out.txt moves stdout to the file first, so 2>&1 copies that -- nothing is left to reach the pipe
---
q: {what does this print?|call it -- what comes out?|read it: what does it print?}
predict: : > f; ls f nope 2>&1 > out.txt | wc -l
a: 1
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: 2>&1 fires first and copies stdout's old target, the pipe -- > out.txt then only moves stdout, leaving the error behind in it
